WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
A researcher requests an export of a large
experiment dataset.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:10.465
Waiting for every file to be prepared inside the
initial request could exceed a useful response

00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:11.065
deadline.

00:00:11.065 --> 00:00:17.397
Instead, the service validates the request, records
an export job and returns a job identifier.

00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:22.862
That response means the service accepted
responsibility for attempting the export.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:29.103
It does not mean a downloadable archive already
exists, so the user interface should display queued

00:00:29.103 --> 00:00:31.358
or processing rather than completed.

00:00:31.358 --> 00:00:35.994
The client can later query the job record for
success or a failure reason.

00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:41.821
A webhook is another option for a client with a
reachable endpoint, but it introduces its own

00:00:41.821 --> 00:00:43.951
authentication and retry contract.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.996
A phone behind a changing network cannot simply be
assumed to accept callbacks.

00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:54.372
Include a terminal failure state so that accepted
jobs do not appear pending forever.

00:00:54.372 --> 00:01:00.064
Trace a worker failure after acceptance and identify
how another worker discovers the job.

00:01:00.064 --> 00:01:05.377
The shorter response time moves completion tracking
into explicit application state.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.695
A document service places a preview-generation
request on a queue.

00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:15.746
The message contains a job identifier, document
identifier, desired representation and schema

00:01:15.746 --> 00:01:16.267
version.

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:22.644
These fields are an API contract even though no
synchronous response carries the finished preview.

00:01:22.644 --> 00:01:28.240
The worker must know which document version to
process and where to record the result.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:34.465
A queue containing only an ambiguous instruction
such as generate preview leaves important business

00:01:34.465 --> 00:01:35.911
decisions to guesswork.

00:01:35.911 --> 00:01:41.381
Consider a worker that stores the preview and
crashes before acknowledging the message.

00:01:41.381 --> 00:01:44.336
Redelivery may cause the same job to run again.

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:50.497
Use the stable job identifier and a suitable result-
update rule so that repetition does not create

00:01:50.497 --> 00:01:52.321
conflicting visible outcomes.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:58.119
Acknowledgement timing should reflect the actual
persistence boundary, not merely receipt into

00:01:58.119 --> 00:01:58.549
memory.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:04.329
Likewise, an in-process queue lost during a restart
provides a different contract from durable

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:05.251
broker storage.

00:02:05.251 --> 00:02:11.276
The architecture should state what survives each
failure and which component owns recovery, rather

00:02:11.276 --> 00:02:14.042
than treating every queue icon as equivalent.
